//!
//! # Shared
//!
//! `Arc<Mutex<T>>` under a shorter name.
//!
//! Sharing one mutable value between threads means an `Arc` around a `Mutex`
//! at every site that passes it, so the standard library declares the pair
//! once. The alias is transparent: `Shared<Store>` is `Arc<Mutex<Store>>`
//! wherever it is written, in a local annotation, a type argument or the
//! receiver of a method call, and the bounds of `Arc` and `Mutex` still apply
//! to `Store`.
//!
//! Spell `Arc<Mutex<T>>` out instead when the surrounding code is
//! lock- and guard-heavy enough that naming which half owns the count and
//! which half owns the value reads clearer.
//!
//! ## Example
//!
//! ```ignis
//! import Arc, Mutex, Shared from "std::sync";
//!
//! function main(): i32 {
//!   let counter: Shared<u64> = Arc::new<Mutex<u64>>(Mutex::new<u64>(0));
//!   let other: Shared<u64> = counter.clone();
//!
//!   return *other.get().lock().read() as i32;
//! }
//! ```

import Arc from "./arc";
import Mutex from "./mutex";

export type Shared<T> = Arc<Mutex<T>>;
